# {{ distribution_name }}

An independent Python project generated from a trusted AgentReady template.

## Getting started

Generate without preparing an environment with `agentready init {{ distribution_name }} --profile {{ profile }}`. From the generated directory, run `python scripts/project.py bootstrap` to synchronize dependencies, create `uv.lock`, and run the regular project checks.

Alternatively, `agentready init {{ distribution_name }} --profile {{ profile }} --bootstrap` performs that preparation and the read-only AgentReady doctor during generation. A failed bootstrap leaves the generated repository in place for inspection and repair.

From the project directory, run `python scripts/project.py check` for registry, architecture, codebase-map freshness, format, lint, type, and test checks. Run `python scripts/project.py verify` for those checks plus a package build.

`python scripts/project.py status` gives a read-only health summary; `python scripts/project.py context --next` points to the next READY specification, skill, workflow, and relevant standards. Use `python scripts/project.py module new <name> --responsibility "..."` when a genuinely new capability needs a module, then `python scripts/project.py sync` to regenerate indexes and the map. `python scripts/project.py package-check` builds and smoke-tests an installed wheel offline in a temporary environment. `check` and `verify` use the existing lock without relocking; `bootstrap` is the dependency-sync step.

## Developer navigation

Use `python scripts/project.py skill check` for Skill Contract v1 and
`python scripts/project.py skill eval feature-builder` for lexical trigger fixtures. Behavioral
observations come from an external run via `--results`; no model is invoked.
`python scripts/project.py checkpoint W001` records durable state and
`python scripts/project.py resume W001` reports staleness. Explicit state input uses `--state`;
existing checkpoints require `--replace`.

Hooks are disabled by default and require explicit activation. AgentReady does not change global
agent configuration. Inspect `python scripts/project.py hooks list`, preview with `hooks enable
safe-shell --dry-run`, then activate explicitly. A developer or host invokes `hooks check` to
preflight a command; it never executes commands or installs automatic interception. Config,
evidence, checkpoints and stdlib scripts remain usable after detach. See agent guidance below.

